          "title": "1.2 Units, Measurements, and Experimental Design",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/7663144/pexels-photo-7663144.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zKXKALOU1yY",
          "content": "Measurements in Science:\nScience is a quantitative endeavor, relying on precise and accurate measurements of physical and chemical quantities.\n\nPhysical Quantity: A characteristic or property of an object or phenomenon that can be measured (e.g., length, mass, time, temperature, volume, density).\n\nUnit: A standard of comparison for measurements of a physical quantity. A measurement consists of a numerical value and a unit.\n\nInternational System of Units (SI Units): The modern form of the metric system and the globally accepted standard system of units for science, technology, and commerce.\n\nSeven Base SI Units:\n\nLength: meter (m)\n\nMass: kilogram (kg)\n\nTime: second (s)\n\nElectric Current: ampere (A)\n\nThermodynamic Temperature: kelvin (K)\n\nAmount of Substance: mole (mol)\n\nLuminous Intensity: candela (cd)\n\nDerived SI Units: Formed by combinations of base units.\n\nExamples:\n\nArea: square meter (m²)\n\nVolume: cubic meter (m³) (Liter, L, is also commonly used: 1 L = 0.001 m³ = 1000 cm³)\n\nSpeed/Velocity: meter per second (m/s)\n\nAcceleration: meter per second squared (m/s²)\n\nDensity: kilogram per cubic meter ( kg/m ³)\n\nForce: newton (N) (1 N = 1 kg⋅m/s²)\n\nEnergy/Work: joule (J) (1 J = 1 N⋅m)\n\nPressure: pascal (Pa) (1 Pa = 1 N/m²)\n\nMetric Prefixes: Used with SI units to denote multiples or submultiples of ten, making it easier to express very large or very small quantities (e.g., kilo- (k, 10³), centi- (c, 10⁻²), milli- (m, 10⁻³), micro- (µ, 10⁻⁶), nano- (n, 10⁻⁹)).\n\nExperimental Design:\nThe careful planning of an experiment to ensure that the data collected is relevant, reliable, and can validly test a hypothesis. Key elements:\n\nIdentifying Variables:\n\nIndependent Variable (Manipulated Variable): The factor that is deliberately changed or manipulated by the experimenter to see its effect.\n\nDependent Variable (Responding Variable): The factor that is measured or observed in response to changes in the independent variable. Its value \"depends\" on the independent variable.\n\nControlled Variables (Constants): Factors that are kept the same (constant) throughout all trials of an experiment. Controlling these variables ensures that any observed changes in the dependent variable are likely due to the manipulation of the independent variable, not other factors.\n\nFormulating a Clear Hypothesis and Prediction: (As in 1.1)\n\nControl Group vs. Experimental Group(s):\n\nExperimental Group: The group(s) in an experiment that receive the treatment or manipulation of the independent variable.\n\nControl Group: A group that is treated identically to the experimental group(s) in all respects except that it does not receive the experimental treatment (or receives a standard treatment or placebo). The control group serves as a baseline for comparison to determine if the independent variable actually had an effect.\n\nReplication and Sample Size:\n\nReplication: Repeating the experiment multiple times (multiple trials) under the same conditions to ensure reliability and reduce the impact of random errors.\n\nSample Size: The number of subjects or individual items being tested in each group. A larger sample size generally increases the reliability and statistical significance of the results.\n\nProcedure: A detailed, step-by-step description of how the experiment will be conducted. It should be clear enough for someone else to replicate the experiment.\n\nData Collection Plan: How data for the dependent variable (and any other relevant measurements) will be collected, recorded, and organized (e.g., in tables).\n\nFair Test: An experiment is a \"fair test\" if only one independent variable is changed at a time, and all other potentially influencing variables are kept constant (controlled). This allows for valid conclusions about cause and effect.\n\n(Suggested insertion: A table summarizing the seven SI base units and their quantities. A table of common metric prefixes. A hypothetical simple experimental design identifying independent, dependent, and controlled variables, and outlining control/experimental groups.)",
