          "title": "1.3 Laboratory Safety and Equipment",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/5726837/pexels-photo-5726837.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fgJd2D4i524",
          "content": "Working in a science laboratory involves potential hazards, so adherence to safety rules and proper use of equipment are paramount.\nGeneral Laboratory Safety Rules:\n\nPreparation: Read the lab procedure thoroughly before starting. Understand potential hazards and safety precautions.\n\nPersonal Protective Equipment (PPE): Always wear appropriate PPE as instructed. This typically includes:\n\nSafety goggles or glasses (to protect eyes from splashes, fumes, projectiles).\n\nLab coat or apron (to protect skin and clothing).\n\nGloves (if handling chemicals or biological specimens).\n\nClosed-toe shoes (no sandals).\n\nTie back long hair.\n\nGeneral Conduct:\n\nNo eating, drinking, smoking, or applying cosmetics in the lab.\n\nNever taste or directly smell chemicals. Waft fumes towards your nose if instructed.\n\nMaintain a clean and organized workspace.\n\nWork responsibly; no horseplay or unauthorized experiments.\n\nReport all accidents, injuries, spills, or broken equipment to the instructor immediately, no matter how minor.\n\nHanlding Chemicals:\n\nAssume all chemicals are hazardous unless known otherwise.\n\nRead labels carefully. Know the hazards (e.g., flammable, corrosive, toxic) by looking for hazard symbols.\n\nAlways add acid to water slowly while stirring (never water to acid) to dissipate heat.\n\nUse a fume hood when working with volatile or toxic substances.\n\nDispose of chemical waste properly according to instructions (not down the sink unless permitted).\n\nHandling Glassware:\n\nCheck glassware for cracks or chips before use; do not use damaged glassware.\n\nHandle with care to avoid breakage.\n\nWhen heating glassware, use appropriate methods (e.g., wire gauze on a Bunsen burner stand) and ensure it is heat-resistant (e.g., Pyrex). Do not heat volumetric glassware.\n\nAllow hot glassware to cool before handling.\n\nDispose of broken glassware in designated sharps containers.\n\nHeating Substances:\n\nNever leave a Bunsen burner or hot plate unattended.\n\nPoint test tubes being heated away from yourself and others.\n\nUse tongs or heat-resistant gloves to handle hot objects.\n\nUsing Equipment: Use all equipment only as instructed and for its intended purpose.\n\nElectrical Safety: Keep water away from electrical equipment. Ensure hands are dry. Report frayed cords or malfunctioning equipment.\n\nFirst Aid and Emergencies: Know the location of:\n\nFirst aid kit.\n\nEyewash station and safety shower.\n\nFire extinguisher and fire blanket.\n\nEmergency exits.\n\nClean-up: Clean all equipment and your workspace thoroughly after use. Wash hands before leaving the lab.\n\nCommon Laboratory Equipment (Brief Introduction – more details in specific discipline sections/practicals):\n\nMeasuring Volume: Beakers, flasks (Erlenmeyer, volumetric), graduated cylinders, pipettes, burettes. (Accuracy varies).\n\nMeasuring Mass: Balances (e.g., triple beam balance, electronic/digital balance).\n\nMeasuring Temperature: Thermometers (alcohol, mercury - less common now, digital).\n\nHeating: Bunsen burner, hot plate, water bath.\n\nHolding/Supporting: Test tubes, test tube rack, clamps, ring stand, retort stand.\n\nObserving: Magnifying glass, microscope.\n\nOther: Watch glass, evaporating dish, crucible, funnel, stirring rod, spatula.\n\n(Suggested insertion: Clear images of common laboratory hazard symbols (e.g., flammable, corrosive, toxic, irritant, oxidizer, biohazard). Pictures of key PPE (goggles, lab coat, gloves). Images of commonly used basic lab equipment with brief labels identifying them.)",
