          "title": "2.4 Mixtures, Solutions, and Separation Techniques",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/7663144/pexels-photo-7663144.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=67Q7_eA-1Og",
          "content": "Mixtures:\nCombinations of two or more pure substances (elements or compounds) that are physically mixed together but not chemically bonded.\n\nThe substances in a mixture retain their individual properties.\n\nThe composition of a mixture can vary (not fixed proportions).\n\nMixtures can usually be separated into their components by physical means.\n\nTypes of Mixtures:\n\nHomogeneous Mixture (Solution):\n\nHas a uniform composition and properties throughout. The different components are not visually distinguishable (one phase).\n\nExample: Saltwater (salt dissolved in water), air (mixture of gases), sugar dissolved in tea, alloys (solid solutions of metals, e.g., brass - copper and zinc).\n\nA solution is a homogeneous mixture where one substance (the solute) is dissolved uniformly in another substance (the solvent).\n\nSolute: Substance being dissolved (usually in smaller amount).\n\nSolvent: Substance doing the dissolving (usually in larger amount).\n\nWater is often called the \"universal solvent.\"\n\nHeterogeneous Mixture:\n\nDoes not have a uniform composition throughout. The different components are often visually distinguishable, and properties may vary from one part of the mixture to another (multiple phases).\n\nExamples: Sand and water, oil and water, soil, a salad, concrete, granite.\n\nTypes of Heterogeneous Mixtures:\n\nSuspension: Heterogeneous mixture containing larger particles that are temporarily suspended in a fluid but will eventually settle out if left undisturbed (e.g., muddy water, some salad dressings). Particles may be visible to naked eye and can be filtered.\n\nColloid: Heterogeneous mixture with dispersed particles larger than those in a solution but smaller than those in a suspension. Particles do not settle out readily and are not easily filtered. Often appear cloudy or opaque. Exhibit Tyndall effect (scattering of light). (e.g., milk, fog, mayonnaise, Jell-O, smoke).\n\n(Suggested insertion: A table comparing homogeneous and heterogeneous mixtures with examples. Images showing the Tyndall effect in a colloid vs. no effect in a solution.)\n\nSeparation Techniques for Mixtures:\nMethods used to separate the components of a mixture based on differences in their physical properties, without changing their chemical identity.\n\nFiltration: Separates an insoluble solid from a liquid (or gas) by passing the mixture through a filter medium (e.g., filter paper) that retains the solid (residue) and allows the liquid (filtrate) to pass through.\n\nUsed for: Separating sand from water.\n\nEvaporation: Separates a dissolved solid (solute) from a liquid solvent by heating the solution to evaporate the solvent, leaving the solid behind.\n\nUsed for: Obtaining salt from saltwater.\n\nDistillation (Simple and Fractional): Separates components of a liquid mixture based on differences in their boiling points.\n\nSimple Distillation: Used to separate a liquid solvent from a dissolved solid (if you want to collect the solvent), or to separate two liquids with significantly different boiling points. The liquid with the lower boiling point vaporizes first, the vapor is then cooled and condensed back into a liquid (distillate) and collected.\n\nFractional Distillation: Used to separate a mixture of two or more liquids with boiling points that are relatively close. Uses a fractionating column to achieve better separation. (e.g., separating components of crude oil, separating ethanol from water).\n\nDecantation: Carefully pouring off a liquid from a mixture after an insoluble solid has settled to the bottom (or a less dense immiscible liquid floats on top).\n\nUsed for: Separating sand from water (after settling), oil from water.\n\nCentrifugation: Speeds up the settling of suspended particles (often very fine) by spinning the mixture at high speed in a centrifuge. Denser particles move to the bottom.\n\nUsed for: Separating blood cells from plasma, separating cream from milk.\n\nSeparating Funnel: Used to separate two immiscible liquids (liquids that do not mix, e.g., oil and water) based on their different densities. The denser liquid forms the lower layer and can be drained off.\n\nChromatography (e.g., Paper Chromatography): Separates components of a mixture (e.g., pigments in ink) based on their different affinities for a stationary phase (e.g., filter paper) and a mobile phase (a solvent) that moves through the stationary phase. Components move at different rates and separate.\n\nMagnetic Separation: Uses a magnet to separate magnetic substances (e.g., iron filings) from non-magnetic substances.\n\nSieving: Separates solid particles of different sizes by passing them through a sieve (a mesh or screen with specific sized holes).\n\n(Suggested insertion: Simple diagrams illustrating filtration, simple distillation, separating funnel, paper chromatography. Brief descriptions of when each technique is most suitable.)",
