          "title": "3.1 Atomic Structure (Protons, Neutrons, Electrons)",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/13014236/pexels-photo-13014236.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KwOHJbE4Tro",
          "content": "All matter is composed of incredibly tiny particles called atoms. Atoms are the fundamental building blocks of elements and, through chemical bonding, they form molecules and compounds. Understanding the structure of atoms and how they interact is key to understanding all of chemistry and many aspects of physics and biology. The Periodic Table is a powerful organizational tool that classifies elements based on their atomic structure and recurring properties. This chapter explores atomic structure, isotopes and ions, electronic configuration, trends in the periodic table, and the different types of chemical bonds that hold atoms together.\n\nAtom: The smallest particle of an element that retains the chemical identity of that element. Atoms are incredibly small (typically around 10⁻¹⁰ m in diameter).\nEarly models of the atom (e.g., Dalton's indivisible sphere, Thomson's \"plum pudding\" model) have been superseded by the modern nuclear model based on experiments like Rutherford's gold foil experiment.\n\nModern Atomic Model (Simplified Nuclear Model):\nAn atom consists of two main regions:\n\nNucleus:\n\nA very small, dense, positively charged core at the center of the atom. Contains most of the atom's mass.\n\nComposed of two types of nucleons:\n\nProtons (p⁺): Positively charged subatomic particles. Charge +1e (where e is the elementary charge, ~1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ C). Relative mass ≈ 1 atomic mass unit (amu).\n\nNeutrons (n⁰): Neutral (no charge) subatomic particles. Relative mass ≈ 1 amu (slightly more massive than a proton).\n\nElectron Cloud (Electron Shells):\n\nThe region surrounding the nucleus where electrons (e⁻) are found. Electrons are much smaller and lighter than protons and neutrons.\n\nElectrons (e⁻): Negatively charged subatomic particles. Charge -1e. Relative mass ≈ 1/1836 amu (very small).\n\nElectrons orbit the nucleus in specific energy levels or shells, not in fixed paths like planets (more accurately described by quantum mechanics as probability clouds or orbitals – see 3.2).\n\nIn a neutral atom, the number of electrons is equal to the number of protons, so the overall charge is zero.\n\nKey Atomic Numbers:\n\nAtomic Number (Z):\n\nThe number of protons in the nucleus of an atom.\n\nDefines the element. All atoms of a particular element have the same atomic number.\n\nIn a neutral atom, Z = number of protons = number of electrons.\n\nThe Periodic Table is arranged in order of increasing atomic number.\n\nMass Number (A) (or Nucleon Number):\n\nThe total number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus of an atom.\n\nA = Number of protons + Number of neutrons = Z + N (where N is number of neutrons).\n\nNumber of neutrons (N) = A - Z.\n\nNotation for an Atom (Nuclide Symbol):\nᴬ_ZX\nwhere X is the chemical symbol of the element, A is the mass number (superscript, left), and Z is the atomic number (subscript, left).\n\nExample: Carbon-12 is ¹²₆C (6 protons, 6 electrons in neutral atom, 12-6 = 6 neutrons). Uranium-238 is ²³⁸₉₂U (92 protons, 238-92 = 146 neutrons).\n\n(Suggested insertion: A simplified diagram of a Bohr-like model of an atom (e.g., Helium), showing nucleus with protons and neutrons, and electrons in shells. A table summarizing the properties (charge, relative mass, location) of protons, neutrons, and electrons.)",
