          "title": "4.1 Types of Chemical Reactions (Combination, Decomposition, Displacement, etc.)",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/5726695/pexels-photo-5726695.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aMU1RaRulSo",
          "content": "Chemistry is largely the study of how matter changes. Chemical reactions are processes that involve the rearrangement of atoms and molecules to form new substances with different properties. Stoichiometry (from Greek \"stoicheion\" = element, \"metron\" = measure) is the branch of chemistry that deals with the quantitative relationships between reactants and products in chemical reactions. This chapter explores types of chemical reactions, how to write and balance chemical equations, the mole concept for measuring amounts of substances, and calculations involving limiting reagents, percent yield, and solution concentrations.\n\nChemical reactions can be classified in various ways. Some common types based on the pattern of reactant/product transformation include:\n\nCombination (Synthesis) Reaction:\n\nTwo or more simpler substances (elements or compounds) combine to form a single, more complex compound.\n\nGeneral form: A + B → AB\n\nExamples:\n\n2Mg(s) + O₂(g) → 2MgO(s) (Magnesium burns in oxygen to form magnesium oxide)\n\nC(s) + O₂(g) → CO₂(g) (Carbon burns to form carbon dioxide)\n\nNH₃(g) + HCl(g) → NH₄Cl(s) (Ammonia gas reacts with hydrogen chloride gas to form solid ammonium chloride)\n\nDecomposition Reaction:\n\nA single compound breaks down into two or more simpler substances (elements or smaller compounds). Often requires energy input (e.g., heat, light, electricity).\n\nGeneral form: AB → A + B\n\nExamples:\n\n2H₂O(l) → 2H₂(g) + O₂(g) (Electrolysis of water)\n\nCaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g) (Heating calcium carbonate/limestone)\n\n2KClO₃(s) → 2KCl(s) + 3O₂(g) (Heating potassium chlorate)\n\nSingle Displacement (Single Replacement) Reaction:\n\nOne element replaces another element in a compound.\n\nOften involves a more reactive metal displacing a less reactive metal from its salt solution, or a more reactive halogen displacing a less reactive halogen. Reactivity series are used to predict these.\n\nGeneral forms:\n\nA + BC → AC + B (If A is a metal displacing B, or a halogen displacing another halogen)\n\nA + BC → BA + C (If A is a non-metal, like hydrogen being displaced by a metal from an acid)\n\nExamples:\n\nZn(s) + CuSO₄(aq) → ZnSO₄(aq) + Cu(s) (Zinc displaces copper)\n\nCl₂(g) + 2KBr(aq) → 2KCl(aq) + Br₂(l) (Chlorine displaces bromine)\n\nMg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g) (Magnesium displaces hydrogen from acid)\n\nDouble Displacement (Double Replacement or Metathesis) Reaction:\n\nThe positive ions (cations) and negative ions (anions) of two ionic compounds exchange partners to form two new compounds.\n\nOften occurs in aqueous solutions. A double displacement reaction typically proceeds if one of the products is:\n\nAn insoluble precipitate (solid).\n\nA gas that bubbles out.\n\nA molecular compound like water (e.g., in neutralization).\n\nGeneral form: AB + CD → AD + CB\n\nExamples:\n\nAgNO₃(aq) + NaCl(aq) → AgCl(s) + NaNO₃(aq) (Precipitation of silver chloride)\n\nHCl(aq) + NaOH(aq) → NaCl(aq) + H₂O(l) (Acid-base neutralization)\n\nNa₂S(aq) + 2HCl(aq) → 2NaCl(aq) + H₂S(g) (Formation of a gas)\n\nOther Important Types of Reactions:\n\nCombustion Reaction: A rapid reaction (usually with oxygen) that produces heat and light (flame). Typically involves organic compounds (containing carbon and hydrogen) burning in oxygen to produce carbon dioxide (CO₂) and water (H₂O).\n\nExample: CH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(g) (Combustion of methane)\n\nAcid-Base Neutralization Reaction: Reaction between an acid and a base, usually producing a salt and water. (A type of double displacement).\n\nExample: HCl(aq) + NaOH(aq) → NaCl(aq) + H₂O(l)\n\nRedox (Oxidation-Reduction) Reactions: Reactions involving the transfer of electrons between chemical species.\n\nOxidation: Loss of electrons (or increase in oxidation state).\n\nReduction: Gain of electrons (or decrease in oxidation state).\n\nMany of the above reaction types (e.g., combination involving elements, single displacement, combustion) are also redox reactions.\n\n(Suggested insertion: For each reaction type, provide a clear definition, general form, and several balanced chemical equation examples. Highlight how to identify each type. A note on the activity series for single displacement.)",
