          "title": "4.4 Limiting Reagents, Percent Yield, and Concentration Units",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/7663144/pexels-photo-7663144.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jtAj0s203CI",
          "content": "Limiting Reagent (or Limiting Reactant):\nIn a chemical reaction with multiple reactants, the limiting reagent is the reactant that is completely consumed first, thereby limiting the amount of product that can be formed. Other reactants are said to be in excess.\n\nTo identify the limiting reagent:\n\nCalculate the number of moles of each reactant present.\n\nDivide the moles of each reactant by its stoichiometric coefficient in the balanced equation.\n\nThe reactant that gives the smallest value is the limiting reagent.\n\nThe amount of product formed is determined by the amount of the limiting reagent. All stoichiometric calculations for product yield should be based on the limiting reagent.\n\nPercent Yield:\nCompares the actual amount of product obtained in an experiment (actual yield) to the maximum amount that could theoretically be produced based on stoichiometry (theoretical yield).\n\nTheoretical Yield: The maximum amount of product that can be formed from a given amount of reactants, calculated using stoichiometry (assuming 100% reaction efficiency and no losses).\n\nActual Yield: The amount of product actually obtained when the reaction is carried out in the laboratory. Often less than theoretical yield due to side reactions, incomplete reactions, losses during separation/purification.\n\nPercent Yield = (Actual Yield / Theoretical Yield) × 100%\n\n(Suggested insertion: A worked example clearly showing how to identify the limiting reagent from given masses of reactants, and then how to calculate the theoretical yield of a product. An example calculation of percent yield given actual and theoretical yields.)\n\nConcentration of Solutions:\nMany reactions occur in solution. Concentration describes the amount of solute dissolved in a given amount of solvent or solution.\n\nMolarity (M):\n\nThe number of moles of solute per liter of solution.\n\nMolarity (M) = Moles of solute (mol) / Volume of solution (L)\n\nUnits: mol/L or M.\n\nExample: A 1 M NaCl solution contains 1 mole of NaCl dissolved in enough water to make 1 liter of solution.\n\nMass Percent (%(w/w) or %(m/m)):\n\n(Mass of solute / Mass of solution) × 100%\n\nVolume Percent (%(v/v)): (Usually for liquid-liquid solutions)\n\n(Volume of solute / Volume of solution) × 100%\n\nMass/Volume Percent (%(w/v) or %(m/v)):\n\n(Mass of solute (g) / Volume of solution (mL)) × 100%\n\nMolality (m): (Less common in Gen Sci, more in Chem)\n\nMoles of solute per kilogram of solvent. Molality (m) = Moles of solute / Mass of solvent (kg).\n\nDilution: The process of reducing the concentration of a solute in solution, usually by adding more solvent.\n\nThe amount of solute remains constant during dilution: Moles_initial = Moles_final.\n\nUsing Molarity: M₁V₁ = M₂V₂\n(where M₁, V₁ are initial molarity and volume; M₂, V₂ are final molarity and volume).\n\n(Suggested insertion: Definitions and formulas for common concentration units. Worked examples of calculating molarity, preparing solutions of a given molarity, and dilution calculations using M₁V₁ = M₂V₂.)",
