          "content": "Physics is the study of matter, energy, and their interactions. Mechanics, a fundamental branch of physics, deals with the motion of objects and the forces that cause or change that motion. This chapter introduces basic concepts from mechanics, including the distinction between scalars and vectors, Newton's laws of motion which form the foundation of classical dynamics, and the important concepts of work, energy, and power. We will also look at simple machines and how they help us do work.\n\n(This section reviews concepts also present in \"General Mathematics\" Ch 1.3 and physics texts Ch 1/2, now contextualized for physical quantities.)\n\nPhysical Quantity: A property of an object or system that can be quantified by measurement (e.g., length, mass, time, velocity, force, energy).\n\nMeasurement: The process of determining the value of a physical quantity. It involves a number and a unit.\n\nUnits (SI Units - Review): Consistent use of Standard International (SI) units is crucial in physics.\n\nBase units: meter (m) for length, kilogram (kg) for mass, second (s) for time.\n\nDerived units: newton (N) for force (kg⋅m/s²), joule (J) for energy/work (N⋅m), watt (W) for power (J/s).\n\nScalar Quantity: A physical quantity that is fully described by its magnitude (a numerical value with a unit) only. It has no direction associated with it.\n\nExamples in Physics: Distance, speed, mass, time, temperature, energy, work, power, density, volume.\n\nScalar arithmetic is ordinary arithmetic.\n\nVector Quantity: A physical quantity that is fully described by both its magnitude and a direction in space.\n\nExamples in Physics: Displacement, velocity, acceleration, force, momentum, impulse, torque, electric field, magnetic field.\n\nRepresented graphically by arrows (length indicates magnitude, arrowhead indicates direction).\n\nVector addition (e.g., tip-to-tail, parallelogram method, component method) and subtraction follow specific rules. (A brief recap here, full detail usually in a dedicated physics vector chapter).\n\nResolution of Vectors: Breaking a vector into perpendicular components (e.g., x and y components in 2D). If F⃗ makes angle θ with x-axis: F_x = F cosθ, F_y = F sinθ.\n\nResultant of components: F = √(F_x² + F_y²), θ = tan⁻¹(F_y/F_x).\n\n(Suggested insertion: Table distinguishing scalars and vectors with examples relevant to physics. Simple diagrams showing vector addition and resolution into components.)",
