          "title": "7.3 DNA, RNA, and Protein Synthesis (Basic Overview)",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/4238510/pexels-photo-4238510.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R5Tv5s8yyYA",
          "content": "Genetic information stored in DNA directs the synthesis of proteins, which carry out most of the cell's functions.\nDNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid):\n\nMolecule of heredity; carries genetic instructions.\n\nStructure: Double helix of two polynucleotide strands. Each nucleotide consists of a deoxyribose sugar, a phosphate group, and one of four nitrogenous bases: Adenine (A), Guanine (G), Cytosine (C), Thymine (T).\n\nComplementary Base Pairing: A pairs with T (A-T), G pairs with C (G-C), held by hydrogen bonds.\n\nSequence of bases forms the genetic code. A gene is a segment of DNA that usually codes for a protein (or an RNA molecule).\n\nLocated in nucleus (eukaryotes) or nucleoid (prokaryotes).\n\nRNA (Ribonucleic Acid):\nPlays crucial roles in expressing the genetic information from DNA into proteins.\n\nStructure: Usually single-stranded polynucleotide. Sugar is ribose. Base Uracil (U) replaces Thymine (T) (A pairs with U in RNA).\n\nTypes of RNA:\n\nMessenger RNA (mRNA): Carries a copy of the genetic code (codons) from a DNA gene in the nucleus to the ribosomes in the cytoplasm.\n\nTransfer RNA (tRNA): \"Adapter\" molecule. Carries a specific amino acid and has an anticodon that base-pairs with an mRNA codon, ensuring correct amino acid sequence during protein synthesis.\n\nRibosomal RNA (rRNA): Major component of ribosomes; helps catalyze peptide bond formation.\n\nCentral Dogma of Molecular Biology: Flow of genetic information:\nDNA → (Transcription) → RNA → (Translation) → Protein\n\nTranscription (in Nucleus for Eukaryotes):\n\nSynthesis of an mRNA molecule complementary to a DNA template strand of a gene.\n\nEnzyme: RNA polymerase.\n\nmRNA carries the genetic message out of the nucleus.\n\nTranslation (on Ribosomes in Cytoplasm):\n\nSynthesis of a polypeptide (protein) using the information encoded in the mRNA codons.\n\nCodon: A sequence of three mRNA bases that specifies a particular amino acid (or a start/stop signal).\n\nRibosomes move along mRNA, tRNAs bring correct amino acids corresponding to mRNA codons, and peptide bonds are formed between amino acids, building a polypeptide chain.\n\nThe sequence of amino acids determines the protein's structure and function.\n\nThis is a highly simplified overview; details of these processes are complex.\n\n(Suggested insertion: Simple diagrams of DNA double helix structure and RNA structure. A flowchart illustrating the central dogma (DNA → RNA → Protein) with brief labels for transcription and translation sites.)",
