          "title": "7.4 Mendelian Genetics: Monohybrid and Dihybrid Crosses",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/25626587/pexels-photo-25626587.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fnZxHBT4gsY",
          "content": "Genetics is the study of heredity – how traits are passed from parents to offspring. Gregor Mendel (19th century) laid the foundations with his experiments on pea plants.\n\nGene: A unit of heredity; a segment of DNA that codes for a specific trait.\n\nAllele: Alternative forms of a gene for a particular trait (e.g., allele for tall plants, allele for short plants). Organisms usually have two alleles for each trait (one from each parent).\n\nGenotype: The genetic makeup of an organism (the combination of alleles it possesses for a trait, e.g., TT, Tt, tt).\n\nPhenotype: The observable physical or biochemical characteristic of an organism, determined by its genotype and environmental influences (e.g., tall plant, short plant).\n\nHomozygous: Having two identical alleles for a trait (e.g., TT - homozygous dominant, or tt - homozygous recessive).\n\nHeterozygous: Having two different alleles for a trait (e.g., Tt).\n\nDominant Allele: An allele whose trait is expressed in the phenotype even if only one copy is present (in a heterozygote). Represented by a capital letter (e.g., T for tall).\n\nRecessive Allele: An allele whose trait is expressed in the phenotype only if two copies are present (homozygous recessive). Masked by a dominant allele in a heterozygote. Represented by a lowercase letter (e.g., t for short).\n\nMendel's Laws:\n\nLaw of Segregation: During gamete (sperm/egg) formation by meiosis, the two alleles for each trait separate (segregate) so that each gamete carries only one allele for that trait.\n\nLaw of Independent Assortment: Alleles of different genes (for different traits, located on different chromosomes) assort independently of one another during gamete formation. (This means inheritance of one trait does not affect inheritance of another, for unlinked genes).\n\nMonohybrid Cross: A genetic cross involving a single trait.\n\nTypically starts with crossing two true-breeding (homozygous) parents for contrasting traits (e.g., Tall TT x Short tt). This is the P (parental) generation.\n\nF₁ (First Filial) Generation: All offspring are heterozygous (Tt) and express the dominant trait (Tall).\n\nF₂ (Second Filial) Generation (from F₁ x F₁ cross, i.e., Tt x Tt):\n\nPunnett Square: A diagram used to predict the possible genotypes and phenotypes of offspring from a cross.\n\nGenotypic Ratio: 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt (or 1:2:1)\n\nPhenotypic Ratio: 3 Tall : 1 Short (or 3:1)\n\nDihybrid Cross: A genetic cross involving two different traits simultaneously (e.g., seed shape and seed color).\n\nExample: True-breeding round yellow seeds (RRYY) x wrinkled green seeds (rryy).\n\nF₁ Generation: All RrYy (round, yellow - if R and Y are dominant).\n\nF₂ Generation (from F₁ x F₁ cross, i.e., RrYy x RrYy): Produces offspring with four different phenotypes in a characteristic ratio of 9:3:3:1 (due to independent assortment):\n\n9 Round Yellow\n\n3 Round Green\n\n3 Wrinkled Yellow\n\n1 Wrinkled Green\n\nThis 9:3:3:1 ratio is expected if the two genes are on different chromosomes and assort independently.\n\nOther Genetic Concepts (Briefly):\n\nIncomplete Dominance: Heterozygote shows an intermediate phenotype (e.g., red x white flowers → pink flowers).\n\nCodominance: Both alleles are fully expressed in the heterozygote (e.g., AB blood type).\n\nSex-linked Traits: Traits determined by genes on sex chromosomes (X or Y).\n\nMutations: Changes in DNA sequence, ultimate source of new alleles.\n\n(Suggested insertion: Example of a Punnett square for a monohybrid cross (Tt x Tt) showing genotypic and phenotypic ratios. Example of a Punnett square for a dihybrid cross (RrYy x RrYy) deriving the 9:3:3:1 ratio.)\n\n(End of Chapter 7)",
