          "title": "8.4 Basic Ecology and Conservation (Ecosystems, Food Chains)",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/6990443/pexels-photo-6990443.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F1UPLAKWHJQ",
          "content": "(Overview here, often expanded in a dedicated Ecology section/unit of Integrated Science, or pure Biology.)\nEcology: The study of interactions between organisms and their environment (biotic and abiotic factors).\n\nLevels of Ecological Organization:\n\nOrganism → Population (group of same species) → Community (all populations in an area) → Ecosystem (community + abiotic environment) → Biome (large regional ecosystem) → Biosphere (all life on Earth).\n\nEcosystem: A community of interacting organisms and their physical environment.\n\nEnergy Flow: Energy flows through an ecosystem, typically starting from sunlight captured by producers.\n\nProducers (Autotrophs): Make their own food (e.g., plants, algae - via photosynthesis).\n\nConsumers (Heterotrophs): Obtain energy by eating other organisms.\n\nPrimary Consumers (Herbivores): Eat producers.\n\nSecondary Consumers (Carnivores/Omnivores): Eat primary consumers.\n\nTertiary Consumers: Eat secondary consumers.\n\nDecomposers (e.g., Bacteria, Fungi): Break down dead organic matter, returning nutrients to the ecosystem.\n\nFood Chain: Linear sequence of who eats whom (e.g., Grass → Grasshopper → Frog → Snake). Shows energy transfer.\n\nFood Web: Interconnected food chains in an ecosystem, showing more complex feeding relationships.\n\nTrophic Levels: Feeding levels in a food chain/web. Energy is lost at each transfer (typically ~90% lost as heat, ~10% transferred to next level).\n\nNutrient Cycling (Biogeochemical Cycles): Essential elements (carbon, nitrogen, water, phosphorus) are cycled between living organisms and the non-living environment.\n\nConservation Biology:\nThe scientific study and management of biodiversity (the variety of life) with the aim of protecting species, their habitats, and ecosystems from excessive rates of extinction and erosion of biotic interactions.\n\nThreats to Biodiversity (Human Impacts - review from Ch 22 if a similar chapter exists):\n\nHabitat loss and fragmentation (deforestation, urbanization).\n\nPollution (air, water, soil).\n\nOverexploitation (overfishing, poaching).\n\nInvasive species.\n\nClimate change.\n\nImportance of Biodiversity:\n\nEcosystem stability and resilience.\n\nEcosystem services (clean air/water, pollination, soil fertility).\n\nSources of food, medicine, raw materials.\n\nEthical, aesthetic, and cultural values.\n\nConservation Strategies:\n\nProtected areas (national parks, reserves).\n\nHabitat restoration.\n\nSpecies conservation programs (captive breeding, reintroduction).\n\nSustainable use of resources.\n\nControlling pollution and invasive species.\n\nAddressing climate change.\n\nEducation and public awareness.\n\n(Suggested insertion: A simple diagram of a food chain and a food web with trophic levels indicated. Pictures illustrating threats to biodiversity (e.g., deforestation) and a positive conservation action (e.g., wildlife reserve or reforestation).)",
