          "title": "9.3 Error Analysis, Uncertainty, and Significant Figures (Applied)",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/4238510/pexels-photo-4238510.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Skl93tu49i8",
          "content": "(Review from Chapter 1.4, with focus on application in practical reporting).\n\nTypes of Errors (Recap):\n\nSystematic Errors: Consistent errors due to faulty equipment or flawed method. Affect accuracy. Hard to detect by repetition alone.\n\nRandom Errors: Unpredictable variations. Affect precision. Can be reduced by repeated measurements and averaging.\n\nUncertainty in Measurements: All measurements have an associated uncertainty due to instrument limitations and estimation.\n\nReading an instrument to appropriate precision (e.g., ruler to ±0.05 cm or ±0.1 cm, graduated cylinder).\n\nRepeating readings helps assess random uncertainty. Calculate mean and perhaps range or standard deviation (if more advanced).\n\nSignificant Figures (Recap of Rules from Ch 1 in Gen Math):\n\nMeasurements should be recorded with the correct number of significant figures reflecting instrument precision.\n\nCalculated results should be reported with a number of significant figures consistent with the least precise measurement used in the calculation. (Review rules for mult/div and add/sub).\n\nPercentage Error (Accuracy Assessment, if true value is known):\nPercentage Error = [|Experimental Value - True Value| / True Value] × 100%\n\nSources of Error in Specific Experiments: For any experiment, students should be able to identify potential sources of both systematic and random errors specific to that setup and procedure.\n\nPrecautions and Improvements: Suggesting ways to minimize errors or improve the accuracy/reliability of an experiment. This includes:\n\nControlling variables more effectively.\n\nUsing more precise instruments.\n\nTaking more readings/larger sample size.\n\nAveraging results.\n\nAvoiding parallax error, zero error.\n\nEnsuring fair testing.\n\n(Suggested insertion: Example scenarios of identifying sources of error in a hypothetical experiment and suggesting specific improvements. A reminder of significant figure rules in calculations with scientific data.)",
