          "title": "10.3 Biology Experiments (Microscopy, Mitosis Slides, Photosynthesis Rates)",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/5726799/pexels-photo-5726799.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7KE7s28cZfM",
          "content": "Using a Light Microscope and Preparing a Wet Mount (e.g., Onion Epidermis or Cheek Cells):\n\nAim: To learn how to use a light microscope correctly and observe basic cell structures.\n\nApparatus: Light microscope, glass slides, coverslips, dropper, water, specimen (onion piece or toothpick for cheek cells), stain (e.g., iodine for onion, methylene blue for cheek cells).\n\nProcedure Outline (Microscope Use):\n\nPlace microscope on stable surface. Clean lenses.\n\nStart with lowest power objective lens. Place slide on stage, secure with clips.\n\nUse coarse adjustment knob to bring stage up until objective is close to slide (while watching from side).\n\nLook through eyepiece, slowly lower stage with coarse adjustment until image comes into rough focus.\n\nUse fine adjustment knob to get sharp focus. Adjust diaphragm and condenser for optimal light.\n\nTo move to higher power, rotate nosepiece. Refocus using only fine adjustment knob.\n\nProcedure Outline (Wet Mount Preparation):\n\nPlace a drop of water (or stain) in center of a clean slide.\n\nObtain thin specimen (e.g., peel thin inner epidermis from onion layer; gently scrape inside of cheek).\n\nPlace specimen in the drop of water/stain.\n\nCarefully lower coverslip at an angle (using a needle) to avoid air bubbles.\n\nObservations: Sketch labeled diagrams of cells observed, noting visible structures (e.g., cell wall, nucleus, cytoplasm for onion; cell membrane, nucleus, cytoplasm for cheek cells). Note magnification.\n\nSafety: Handle slides and coverslips carefully (sharps). Handle stains with care (may stain skin/clothes).\n\nObserving Prepared Slides of Mitosis (e.g., Onion Root Tip):\n\nAim: To identify and describe the different stages of mitosis in plant cells.\n\nApparatus: Light microscope, prepared slide of onion root tip mitosis.\n\nProcedure: Scan slide under low power to find region of actively dividing cells (meristematic region, just behind root cap). Switch to high power to observe individual cells.\n\nObservations: Identify cells in different stages of mitosis (Interphase, Prophase, Metaphase, Anaphase, Telophase) based on chromosome appearance and arrangement. Sketch and label representative cells for each stage.\n\nInvestigating a Factor Affecting the Rate of Photosynthesis (e.g., Light Intensity on an Aquatic Plant like Elodea):\n\nAim: To investigate how light intensity affects the rate of oxygen production during photosynthesis.\n\nApparatus: Elodea sprig, test tube or boiling tube, beaker of water (water bath), lamp (light source), metre rule, stopwatch, (optional: sodium bicarbonate solution to ensure CO₂ supply).\n\nProcedure Outline:\n\nSet up Elodea in a test tube with water (and bicarbonate solution if used). Invert a small funnel over it if collecting O₂ gas, or count bubbles.\n\nPlace a lamp at a measured distance (e.g., 10 cm) from the plant. Allow plant to acclimatize.\n\nCount the number of oxygen bubbles produced per unit time (e.g., per minute) for several minutes, or measure volume of gas collected. This is a measure of photosynthetic rate.\n\nRepeat measurements at different light intensities (by changing lamp distance, e.g., 20cm, 30cm, 40cm, 50cm). Keep other factors (temperature, CO₂ concentration) constant.\n\nData: Table of distance from lamp (cm), light intensity (can be expressed as 1/distance² or by a light meter if available), and rate of O₂ production (bubbles/min or volume/min).\n\nGraph: Plot rate of O₂ production (y-axis) against light intensity or 1/distance² (x-axis).\n\nSafety: Handle electrical equipment with dry hands. Avoid looking directly at bright lamp.\n\nAnalysis: Describe relationship between light intensity and rate of photosynthesis. Identify limiting factors. Discuss sources of error.\n\n(Suggested insertion: Labeled diagram of a light microscope. Sketches of different mitosis stages. Diagram of a setup for investigating photosynthesis rate in Elodea. Sample data tables and expected graph shapes for each.)",
